---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Explore the island" description="Hikes, beaches, museum and food on Røvær." lang="en" page="explore">
  <div class="page">
    <h1 class="page-title">Explore the island</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      Røvær offers nature, history and coastal culture, all within walking distance.
    </p>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Hiking and cycling</h2>
      <img class="section-img" src="/images/Sykkel-500x334.jpg" alt="Cycling on Røvær">
      <p>
        Bråvarden is the island's highest point, 4600 centimetres above sea level,
        with panoramic views over the archipelago. Maps of the hiking trails are
        available at the shop or the cafe. The west side of the island offers
        beautiful and varied nature.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bring a padlock and walk Friarvegen to Kjærlighetsbroa (the Love Bridge),
        where couples attach locks to the fence as a symbol of their love.
        Tip: buy a stainless steel lock, preferably engraved, in town.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Swimming</h2>
      <img class="section-img" src="/images/snorkling-500x333.jpg" alt="Snorkelling at Røvær" loading="lazy">
      <p>
        The two most popular swimming spots are Lothastranda and Grønasvika.
        Lothastranda has tables, a barbecue, shelters, a beach volleyball court
        and toilets. The beach is shallow, making it perfect for crab hunting.
        Crabs can also be caught from the floating dock at Grønasvika, but
        remember to release them afterwards.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Historic sites</h2>
      <img class="section-img" src="/images/Rvr_DSC6137_liten-500x334.jpg" alt="Nature on Røvær" loading="lazy">
      <p>
        At Grønasvika you will find Viking ruins, still a beautiful little spot.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hiltahuset Museum can usually be opened by a knowledgeable guide.
        The museum documents the Røvær tragedy of 1899, in which 30 local
        lives were lost, and the island's history. The tragedy still marks
        the island community. Across from the museum is Skjoldbladbua,
        selling local handmade goods.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Food and accommodation</h2>
      <div class="info-box">
        <h3>Røvær Sjøhus</h3>
        <p>Cafe serving komle (potato dumplings) and other dishes. Open Saturday–Sunday 12:00–16:00, April–September. Extended hours in summer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="info-box">
        <h3>Røvær Kulturhotell</h3>
        <p>
          Group accommodation year-round, with various events throughout the season.
          Book via <a href="https://rovarkulturhotell.no" target="_blank" rel="noopener">rovarkulturhotell.no</a>
          or call 527 15 800.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Røvær Aquaculture Centre</h2>
      <img class="section-img" src="/images/151117-Modul-1C-1920x1015-500x264.jpg" alt="Røvær Aquaculture Centre" loading="lazy">
      <p>
        A compact exhibition offering a simple introduction to big and exciting
        topics such as aquaculture, history, environment and natural conditions.
        Screens, games and activities for all ages. Free admission.
      </p>
      <p>
        More information at
        <a href="https://rovarhavbrukssenter.no" target="_blank" rel="noopener">rovarhavbrukssenter.no</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
